### Opening a PR

Invoked at the end of every other playbook that ships.

Host mapping is in `.cursor/rules/rigor-mode.mdc`. Two writers on one branch each get their own worktree, or `git fetch` and `git reset --hard origin/<branch>` between them.

**Worktree.** Branch off the remote default. Confirm it with `git symbolic-ref refs/remotes/origin/HEAD`. Today that is `main`. `integration/v3-prerelease` is a long-lived branch, not the base, unless that command says otherwise. Cloud agents use the session branch (`cursor/<descriptive-name>-8ca2`) and open a second worktree only when two writers would share one checkout. IDE and CLI agents with unrelated dirty work patch it out, use a fresh worktree, and apply. A snarled branch resets from the default branch and redoes the minimum. Done when the work lives on a clean branch off the default.

**Commits.** Commit liberally. Rebase into small, ordered commits before opening PRs. Each commit is a future PR. Landable, ordered to tell the story. Amend when the fix belongs in a just-made commit. Done when every commit is landable and ordered.

**Prose.** Before review, delete comments that narrate what the code shows. Keep a comment only for a non-obvious why. Write the PR title, PR description, and commit body in the voice of Writing the reply. One word for each action, keep articles, and prefer a plain verb over `-ing`. Done when the diff comment pass has run and the title and body follow that voice.

**Titles.** Conventional Commits. `type(scope): subject`. Types are `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`. Scope is the changed area. Subject is short and imperative. Name a real symbol when one carries the change. No trailing period.

**Descriptions.** The PR body is a briefing, not the lab notebook. The squash commit body is the PR body. If the body would make the squash commit longer than about 40 lines, cut the body.

Use these sections in order. Drop a section when it has nothing to say.

- `## Why`. Intent and approach in one or two short paragraphs.
- `## Scope`. Bullets of real symbols and paths. Both sides of a rename.
- `## Tradeoffs`. Rejected alternatives a reviewer would otherwise ask about.
- `## Blast Radius`. Who or what the change touches, and why it is safe or risky.
- `## Verification`. Each real run path and its outcome. For perf, one primary number with unit in `before → after` form.

After these sections, attach videos or screenshots when they prove a claim. Put lab-notebook detail in a linked artifact outside the body.

**Forge.** This repo is GitHub. Resolve the forge before the first PR operation. A Cursor cloud agent uses the host PR tool (`ManagePullRequest`) when the session requires it, and passes `base_branch` as the remote default. An IDE or CLI agent uses `gh pr create`. Never require Graphite (`gt`). Done when the forge choice is recorded.

**Size and stacks.** Prefer five narrow PRs to one large PR. A stack is a base-branch chain. The root PR targets the default branch. Each child rebases onto its parent's exact tip and its PR targets the parent branch. Create a child with `gh pr create --base <parent-branch>`, or the host PR tool's base field.

**Readiness.** Open ready when the host allows it. IDE and CLI agents omit `--draft`. Cursor cloud agents open draft unless the user asked for ready, and the reply says draft. If a PR still opens as a draft and the user asked for ready, run `gh pr ready <number>`. Run `gh pr view` before you refer to PR status. Done when the view reports the readiness you intended.

**Babysit.** Opening a PR does not start a babysit. Post the URL and keep building.

A subagent that opens a PR runs the interrogate pass and the comment purge. It returns the URL and does not babysit.

**Reply:** the PR URL.
